==================================================
2. PRODUCT PAGE
==================================================

Create a polished product detail layout containing:

- Product image/gallery
- Product name
- Price
- Description
- Availability
- Product options/variants
- Quantity selector
- Add to Cart button
- Order Now button

Keep the visual design consistent with the existing Bantex Trading website.

Do NOT create a completely different product-page design.

==================================================
3. PRODUCT IMAGES
==================================================

Build the image/gallery architecture so a product can eventually have:

- Main image
- Additional images
- Multiple product views

Allow the product data to determine which images are displayed.

Do NOT invent product images.

If there are no real products yet, make sure the product system works with properly structured placeholder/test data without presenting fabricated products as real inventory.

==================================================
4. PRODUCT OPTIONS / VARIANTS
==================================================

This is important.

Products must NOT be restricted to one fixed option type.

The Admin system in a later phase should be able to define options such as:

- Colour
- Size
- Model
- Capacity
- Pack size
- Specification
- Other custom options

For example:

Product A:
Colour → Black / Blue / Red

Product B:
Size → A4 / A5

Product C:
Model → Model 1 / Model 2
Colour → Black / White

The product page should dynamically render whatever options the product contains.

Do NOT hard-code only a colour selector.

==================================================
5. VARIANT LOGIC
==================================================

A selected variant must be treated as part of the product being purchased.

The system should be able to identify:

Product
+
Selected options
+
Quantity

as a unique cart item later.

Do not build the full cart yet, but make the product selection architecture compatible with the future cart.

If different variants eventually have different prices, the data architecture should be capable of supporting that.

==================================================
6. QUANTITY
==================================================

Add a polished quantity selector.

Customers should be able to:

- Increase quantity
- Decrease quantity
- See the current quantity

Prevent invalid quantities such as zero or negative values.

==================================================
7. ADD TO CART
==================================================

Create the Add to Cart interaction/UI.

For this phase, establish the frontend interaction and cart integration point.

The full persistent cart system belongs to Phase 5.

Do not build the complete cart system yet.

The product page should still be architected so Phase 5 can connect the button to the real cart.

==================================================
8. ORDER NOW
==================================================

Create an Order Now button.

The eventual flow will be:

Product
→ Select options
→ Order Now
→ Checkout
→ Stripe
→ Order

For Phase 4, establish the button and navigation/integration point.

Do NOT implement Stripe or checkout yet.

==================================================
9. PRODUCT VALIDATION
==================================================

The page must correctly handle:

- Product not found
- Missing image
- Missing optional description
- Products with no variants
- Products with one option
- Products with multiple options
- Invalid variant combinations

Do not allow customers to add a product with a required option left unselected.

Provide clear feedback when an option must be selected.

==================================================
10. PRODUCT ROUTING
==================================================

Use clean dynamic URLs.

For example:

/stationery/[slug]

/electrical/[slug]

Do not create separate manually coded pages for every product.

The same product-detail component should power both categories.

